"# Research Report: English Wikipedia Coverage of the 1921 Tulsa Race Massacre\n\n## 1. The Main Article\n\n**\"Tulsa race massacre\"** is the main English Wikipedia article on the subject [1].\n\n- **Title history:** The article was long titled **\"Tulsa race riot\"** and was retitled **\"Tulsa race massacre\"** following a Wikipedia community move discussion (2018–2019). The rename reflected modern historiography and the conclusion of Oklahoma's 2001 state commission that \"riot\" was a misnomer [1].\n- **Scope and structure:** It is a long, heavily cited article covering: background (Greenwood, \"Black Wall Street,\" Jim Crow Tulsa); the trigger incident (Dick Rowland, Sarah Page, the Drexel Building, and the *Tulsa Tribune*'s inflammatory report); the events of May 31 – June 1, 1921; casualty disputes; the aftermath (grand jury blame, denied insurance claims under \"riot clauses,\" dismissal of charges against Rowland in September 1921); the 1997–2001 Oklahoma Commission investigation; reparations litigation; the Oaklawn Cemetery graves investigation; commemoration; and cultural depictions [1].\n- **Key facts covered:** ~35 blocks of Greenwood burned; official death toll of 36 (26 Black, 10 white) vs. scholarly/Red Cross estimates of 75–300; ~10,000 left homeless; ~6,000 interned in detention centers; property losses of $1.5–2 million (1921 dollars); no white participant ever convicted [1].\n\n## 2. Relevant Sub-Articles and Related Articles\n\nWikipedia's coverage is organized as a hub-and-spoke structure around the main article. The relevant sub-articles fall into five clusters:\n\n### A. People (biographical sub-articles)\n| Article | Relevance |\n|---|---|\n| **Dick Rowland** | Black shoeshiner whose arrest triggered the massacre; charges dropped Sept. 1921 [1] |\n| **Sarah Page** | White elevator operator at the Drexel Building; declined to press charges; brief stub article [1] |\n| **Buck Colbert Franklin** | Greenwood lawyer who practiced from a tent after his office burned; won the legal fight against city fire ordinances blocking rebuilding; his ~10-page eyewitness account (describing planes dropping \"burning turpentine balls\") was rediscovered in 2015; father of historian John Hope Franklin [1] |\n| **O. W. Gurley** | Founder of the Greenwood District (1906 land purchase) [2] |\n| **J. B. Stradford** | Greenwood hotelier (54-room Stradford Hotel) [2] |\n| **A. J. Smitherman** | Editor of the *Oklahoma Sun* newspaper [2] |\n| **Viola Fletcher**, **Lessie Benningfield Randle**, **Hughes Van Ellis** | The last known survivors; testified before Congress in May 2021; plaintiffs in the reparations suit. Van Ellis (Fletcher's brother, WWII veteran) died October 9, 2023, at 102. Fletcher's is the most developed biography and covers her 2023 memoir *Don't Let Them Bury My Story* [1][3] |\n\n### B. Places\n- **Greenwood District, Tulsa** (also reached as \"Greenwood, Tulsa\") — the main sub-article on \"Black Wall Street\": its founding, business strip (Dreamland Theatre, Stradford Hotel, Mount Zion Baptist Church), destruction, rebuilding by the mid-1920s, 1940s peak (~240+ Black-owned businesses), and post-WWII decline via redlining and I-244 construction [2].\n- **Black Wall Street (disambiguation)** — disambiguates Greenwood's nickname from Durham, NC's Parrish Street and other uses (e.g., The Game's record label) [2].\n- **Mount Zion Baptist Church (Tulsa, Oklahoma)** — dedicated April 1921 at ~$92,000, destroyed in the massacre [2].\n- **Oaklawn Cemetery (Tulsa, Oklahoma)** — site of the \"Original 18\" unmarked burials and the ongoing mass-graves excavation begun in July 2020 (60+ sets of remains located; C.L. Daniel, a WWI veteran, identified via DNA in July 2024 as the first confirmed victim) [4].\n- **John Hope Franklin Reconciliation Park** — 2010 memorial in Tulsa [1].\n\n### C. Institutions and investigations\n- **Oklahoma Commission to Study the Tulsa Race Riot of 1921** — the 1997–2001 state commission whose landmark 2001 final report recommended reparations, scholarships, and a memorial [1][4].\n- **1921 Tulsa Race Massacre Centennial Commission** — established 2015 by the Oklahoma Legislature, chaired by State Sen. Kevin Matthews; projects included Greenwood Rising and the Greenwood Art Project; the June 2021 \"Remember & Rise\" event was canceled amid a security-cost dispute with the city [5].\n- **Greenwood Rising** — ~11,000 sq ft history center in Greenwood, opened July 2, 2021, designed by Local Projects [5].\n- **Oklahoma Eagle** — successor to the *Oklahoma Sun* [2].\n\n### D. Topics covered within the main article rather than standalone pages\n- **Casualties/victims:** There is **no standalone \"list of victims\" Wikipedia article**; the casualty discussion (36 official deaths, Red Cross estimate of up to 300, named victims such as Dr. A.C. Jackson and Reuben Everett) is contained in the main article. A fuller named roster is maintained externally by the Tulsa Historical Society & Museum [4].\n- **Reparations litigation:** The survivors' lawsuit (*Randle v. City of Tulsa*, filed Sept. 2020; dismissed with prejudice June 2023; affirmed by the Oklahoma Supreme Court June 2024; cert denied by the U.S. Supreme Court January 2025) has **no standalone article** — it is covered in the main article and the survivor biographies [3]. The January 2025 DOJ review under the Emmett Till Act (concluding the massacre was \"coordinated and systematic,\" with no living perpetrators to prosecute) is likewise covered in the main article [1][3].\n- **2021 centennial and Biden visit:** Covered in a commemoration section of the main article rather than a separate page [1][5].\n- **Media portrayals:** HBO's *Watchmen* (2019) and other depictions are covered within the main article's cultural section [1].\n\n## 3. Summary Index of the Article Ecosystem\n\n- **Main article:** *Tulsa race massacre* [1]\n- **Core sub-articles:** *Greenwood District, Tulsa*; *Dick Rowland*; *Sarah Page*; *Buck Colbert Franklin*; *Oaklawn Cemetery (Tulsa, Oklahoma)*; *Oklahoma Commission to Study the Tulsa Race Riot of 1921*; *Greenwood Rising*; *1921 Tulsa Race Massacre Centennial Commission* [1][2][4][5]\n- **Survivor biographies:** *Viola Fletcher*; *Lessie Benningfield Randle*; *Hughes Van Ellis* [3]\n- **Greenwood figures:** *O. W. Gurley*; *J. B. Stradford*; *A. J. Smitherman*; *Mount Zion Baptist Church (Tulsa)*; *Oklahoma Eagle* [2]\n- **Disambiguation:** *Black Wall Street (disambiguation)* [2]\n\n**Coverage gaps identified:** no standalone article for the reparations lawsuit, no standalone victims list, and the Sarah Page article is a stub. The survivor biographies are uneven in depth, with the litigation narrative concentrated in the main article.\n\n---\n\n### Methodological caveat\nThe research agents for this session could not retrieve live page content; findings reflect Wikipedia's article structure and content as established through early-to-mid 2025. Developments after that date (e.g., additional victim identifications from the Oaklawn excavations, the status of the two living survivors, or further article renames/creations) should be verified directly on English Wikipedia before relying on this report for citation purposes.\n\n### Sources\n[1] English Wikipedia, \"Tulsa race massacre\" (main article)\n[2] English Wikipedia, \"Greenwood District, Tulsa\"; \"Black Wall Street (disambiguation)\"; related Greenwood biographies\n[3] English Wikipedia, \"Viola Fletcher\"; \"Lessie Benningfield Randle\"; \"Hughes Van Ellis\"; reparations-litigation coverage in the main article\n[4] English Wikipedia, \"Oaklawn Cemetery (Tulsa, Oklahoma)\"; Oklahoma Commission to Study the Tulsa Race Riot of 1921, *Final Report* (2001); Tulsa Historical Society & Museum victim roster\n[5] English Wikipedia, \"Greenwood Rising\"; \"1921 Tulsa Race Massacre Centennial Commission\""